{"items":[{"id":"cap-v2-sequence_algorithms-java-repair-01","problem":"Repair the program below. Implement `public static double[] distances(double centerX, double centerY, String[] nuclei)` in a public final class `Solution`.

Each element of `nuclei` is intended to contain two decimal coordinates separated by exactly one comma, such as `"12.5, 7"`. An element is malformed if it is null, does not contain exactly two fields, has a blank field, contains a value that cannot be parsed as a `double`, or contains a non-finite value. Skip malformed elements. For every valid element, calculate its Euclidean distance from `(centerX, centerY)` using `Math.hypot`. Return the distances in the same relative order as their valid source elements. `nuclei` is non-null, and the center coordinates are finite.

Return a corrected complete `Solution.java`. Use only the Java standard library. Do not access the filesystem, network, clock, or randomness.

Known-bad source:
```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public final class Solution {
    private Solution() {
    }

    public static double[] distances(double centerX, double centerY, String[] nuclei) {
        List<Double> result = new ArrayList<>();
        for (String nucleus : nuclei) {
            double[] point = parsePoint(nucleus);
            if (point == null) {
                break;
            }
            result.add(Math.hypot(point[0] - centerX, point[1] - centerY));
        }

        double[] distances = new double[result.size()];
        for (int i = 0; i < result.size(); i++) {
            distances[i] = result.get(i);
        }
        return distances;
    }

    private static double[] parsePoint(String text) {
        if (text == null) {
            return null;
        }
        String[] fields = text.split(",", -1);
        if (fields.length != 2 || fields[0].trim().isEmpty() || fields[1].trim().isEmpty()) {
            return null;
        }
        try {
            double x = Double.parseDouble(fields[0].trim());
            double y = Double.parseDouble(fields[1].trim());
            if (!Double.isFinite(x) || !Double.isFinite(y)) {
                return null;
            }
            return new double[] {x, y};
        } catch (NumberFormatException exception) {
            return null;
        }
    }
}
